# ============================================================
#  Novi — /etc/services
# ============================================================
#
#  Port numbers and the names programs ask for them by. BusyBox ships
#  no such file, so getservbyport(3) and getservbyname(3) returned
#  nothing on this system and every tool that looks up a port printed
#  a bare number -- `novi-recon ports` most visibly, which is a tool
#  whose whole job is telling you what is listening on a machine.
#
#  CURATED, NOT IANA's WHOLE REGISTRY. That file is ~14,000 lines
#  naming services nobody has run this century, and this is the same
#  call kernel/config-x86_64 makes about Kconfig: a list somebody chose
#  beats a list nobody has read. What is here is what a person doing
#  this work actually meets -- the well-known services, the databases,
#  the management ports, and the handful of high ports that matter
#  because something is usually listening on them.
#
#  A PORT NAME IS NOT A POLICY. RFC 0022's rule stands and this file
#  does not relax it: /etc/novi/firewall.nft names ports as NUMBERS,
#  because a rule that reads `ssh` resolves through whatever this file
#  happens to say on the machine it is applied to -- and a firewall
#  whose meaning depends on a name table is a firewall that means
#  something different on two machines. This file is for READING a
#  port, not for writing one.
#
#  Format: name  port/protocol  [aliases...]
#
#  TWO LIMITS COME FROM MUSL'S PARSER, not from the format, and both
#  fail silently. `__lookup_serv()` and `reverse_services()` each read
#  with `fgets(line, 128, f)`, so a line of 128 bytes or more is split
#  and its tail is parsed as a record of its own; and
#  reverse_services() skips any entry whose name is 32 bytes or longer
#  (`if (p-line > 32) continue`), so a long name still resolves by
#  NAME and stops resolving by PORT. packages/tests/test-services.sh
#  asserts both against the shipped file.
#
#  ALIASES WORK ONE WAY. The forward lookup searches the whole line, so
#  getservbyname("www") finds http; the reverse lookup copies the
#  FIRST field only, so getservbyport(80) is always "http". Put the
#  name you want printed first.
#
#  Sorted by port, then protocol. Nothing parses it in order -- the
#  order is so that a person reading a diff can see a duplicate, which
#  the reverse lookup would otherwise resolve to whichever came first.
# ============================================================

